          "local_context": "There are many ways to protect against threats. These include giving warning of threats, making satellites harder to destroy, separating their functions, spreading them out, and keeping backups. If we put these preparations in the same box as weapons that attack from orbit, the categories get mixed up.\n\nLast is a quiz about the “Outer Space Treaty.” If you write “all space weapons are banned” as your answer, it is not accurate. The treaty bans putting nuclear weapons and other weapons of mass destruction in orbit around Earth, placing them on the Moon or other celestial bodies, or deploying them in outer space in another way. It also bans military bases and forts on celestial bodies, as well as weapons tests and military exercises there.\n\nNow for the final question. Don’t just ask, “Are there weapons in space?” Ask whether they are fired from the ground, placed in orbit, or are preparations to protect against threats. If you can read the labels and tell them apart, you pass this news-sorting test.",
